N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a car's fuel gauge: you decide when to fill up by looking at it, and a wrong gauge leaves you stuck however well you drive. Reading works the same way. The reader <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metamon\">monitors</b> her understanding (\"do I get this yet?\") and <b>regulates</b> her study on that basis: she rereads, asks for help, or stops. Students who regulate their reading better understand more."),
("Why accuracy matters","The loop only helps if the check is right (Winne &amp; Perry, 2000). A wrong check leads to two mistakes: she <b>stops too early</b>, or she <b>rereads the wrong text</b>. How well a reader's judgments of her understanding match her actual test scores is called <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"metacomp\">metacomprehension accuracy</b>. The whole chapter is about this one thing."),
("The plan","Four questions: <b>how</b> is accuracy measured, <b>why</b> is it often poor, <b>what</b> improves it, and <b>does</b> better accuracy lead to better learning? It ends with open questions and advice for teachers. (Our link: this is the compare-and-fix loop of reading 1's information processing view, applied to one text.)"),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("The method","The standard method (Glenberg &amp; Epstein, 1985): read several short texts, rate how well you understood each, then answer an <b>inference question</b> about each text (the answer is not written in the text but follows from it). Accuracy is how well the ratings match the scores."),
("Two kinds of accuracy","Picture two judges at a baking contest. One gives every cake 3 points too many but ranks them in exactly the right order; the other gets the average right but ranks them in reverse. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"absacc\">Absolute accuracy</b>: is your confidence the right <b>size</b>? Measured as bias (average predicted minus average actual). <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"relacc\">Relative accuracy</b>: can you tell <b>which</b> texts you understood better? Measured as a correlation within one person, across her texts."),
("Table 8.1","Four students predict 7, 7, 6, 5, 3. <b>Student 1</b> scores exactly that: perfect on both. <b>Student 2</b> scores 3 lower everywhere: bias +3, but a perfect ranking (+1.0). <b>Student 3</b> has no bias on average, but her ranking is reversed (−1.0): she would reread the wrong texts. <b>Student 4</b> is poor on both. <i>In plain words:</i> you can be wrong about how well you did, yet right about which text you know best, or the reverse."),
("Why keep them apart","The two are independent (orthogonal), so a factor can change one but not the other: domain knowledge affected absolute but not relative accuracy (Griffin, Jee &amp; Wiley, 2009). From here on the chapter studies <b>relative accuracy</b>. Our reasoning: to choose what to reread, you only need to know which text you understood least."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("Judging from cues","You cannot look inside your own head, so you judge your understanding from clues, the way you might judge a cake by its icing. Koriat's (1997) <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"cueutil\">cue-utilization framework</b>: judgments are inferred from <b>cues</b>, and they are accurate only when the cues are <b>diagnostic</b>, meaning they really predict the test. To know which cues are diagnostic, you also need a theory of comprehension (Weaver, 1990; Rawson, Dunlosky &amp; Thiede, 2000)."),
("Three levels of understanding","Kintsch's (1988) construction-integration model: we understand a text at three levels. The <b>surface</b> holds the words; the <b>textbase</b> holds the ideas and their links; the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"sitmod\">situation model</b> connects the ideas to what you already know, so you can draw conclusions. Comprehension tests depend mainly on the situation model (McNamara et al., 1996). So the good cue is \"<b>can I explain this?</b>\", and a poor one is \"<b>it felt easy</b>\"."),
("Which cues readers use","<b>Thiede et al. (2010)</b> asked college students which cues they used. Five types came up: being able to explain, being able to recall, prior knowledge, interest, and features of the text. <b>Found:</b> students who judged by whether they could understand or explain the text were more accurate; those who relied on text features were less accurate. <i>In plain words:</i> \"can I explain it?\" works; \"it was short and easy\" misleads."),
("Diagnosticity versus utilization","<b>van Loon et al. (2014)</b> measured each cue's <b>diagnosticity</b> (how well it predicts the test) and <b>utilization</b> (how strongly it drives the judgment). <b>Found:</b> differences in accuracy came more from utilization than from diagnosticity. <i>In plain words:</i> accuracy depends mostly on which cues readers lean on."),
("So what","To help readers judge better, steer them to the right cue. The chapter shows two ways: <b>retrieve before judging</b> (step 4) and <b>read in a way that builds the situation model</b> (step 5)."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","The <b>modified-feedback hypothesis</b> (Glenberg et al., 1987): your judgment reflects how much you can <b>retrieve</b> at the moment you judge. So, before rating your understanding, get the text back out of your head: a short summary, five keywords, or a cause-and-effect diagram. What comes back is a fairer cue than a feeling."),
("Why the delay matters","Activation theories (Fletcher, van den Broek &amp; Arthur, 1996): right after reading, much of a text is still active in working memory, even a text you did not understand, so all texts feel alike: the cues are <b>homogeneous</b>. After a delay, only long-term memory is left: little comes back for weak texts, much for strong ones. The cues become <b>heterogeneous</b>, and long-term memory is also what the test draws on. Our example: right after an album you can hum every song; later, only the ones that stuck."),
("Summaries","<b>Thiede and Anderson (2003):</b> six texts. One group summarized each text right after reading it, one read all six first and then summarized, and a control group wrote nothing. <b>Found:</b> the delayed-summary group was the most accurate. <b>Anderson and Thiede (2008):</b> summaries helped both typical and at-risk readers, but at-risk readers relied more often on surface cues. <i>In plain words:</i> summarize later, not straight away."),
("Keywords and diagrams","<b>Thiede et al. (2003):</b> five keywords per text, written after a delay, gave the most accurate judgments (replicated by Chen, 2009). <b>Thiede et al. (2005):</b> the crucial gap is between reading and writing the keywords. <b>van Loon et al. (2014):</b> completing a cause-and-effect diagram after a delay beat completing it immediately or making none. <i>In plain words:</i> same rule, quicker tasks."),
("So what","Test yourself before judging, but not straight away. This has worked for college students and down to <b>grade 6</b>, but <b>may not work in grade 4</b> (de Bruin et al., 2011). The second route changes how students read (step 5)."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","The second route changes <b>how</b> you read. If reading builds the situation model, its cue (\"can I explain this?\") is right there when you judge yourself. Think of furniture: assembling it yourself shows which joints you understood; looking at someone else's finished piece does not."),
("Concept mapping","A <b>concept map</b> is a drawing of a text's ideas and how they connect. <b>Thiede et al. (2010, Experiment 2):</b> at-risk readers judged more accurately when they made maps. <b>Redford et al. (2012):</b> 7th graders who made maps judged better, but those <b>given ready-made maps</b> were no better than pupils without maps. <i>In plain words:</i> the making is what helps."),
("Self-explanation","<b>Self-explaining</b> (Chi, 2000) means telling yourself what each sentence means and how it fits the whole. <b>Griffin, Wiley and Thiede (2008):</b> college students who self-explained judged more accurately than students who read the text twice in the same time, or once. <i>In plain words:</i> explaining, not extra time, makes the difference."),
("Test expectancy","<b>Thiede, Griffin and Wiley (2011):</b> after practice texts with either fact tests or understanding tests, students rated new texts. <b>Found:</b> expecting understanding tests raised <b>metacomprehension</b> accuracy; expecting fact tests raised <b>metamemory</b> accuracy (predicting memory for facts). Thiede et al. (2012) found the same in grades 7 and 8. <i>In plain words:</i> what you expect the test to ask changes how you read and judge."),
("So what","Making your own map, explaining to yourself and expecting a deep test all point readers to situation-model cues. Step 6 asks whether better judging actually leads to better learning."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("The idea","The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"discred\">discrepancy-reduction model</b> (Dunlosky &amp; Hertzog, 1997): students keep studying until the gap between \"how well I know it\" and \"how well I want to know it\" is closed, so the bigger the gap, the more restudy. Much data fits this (Son &amp; Kornell, 2008), and it appears to make study time most effective (Tullis &amp; Benjamin, 2011)."),
("Regulation is already there","Students are fairly good at acting on their judgments. Pupils in grades 4, 6 and 7 picked their less-learned texts for restudy (de Bruin et al., 2011), at-risk readers did too (Anderson &amp; Thiede, 2008), and even grade-1 children gave harder items more time (Dufresne &amp; Kobasigawa, 1989). Some still find it hard to turn monitoring into action (Sussan &amp; Son, 2014)."),
("The chain is shown","<b>Thiede et al. (2003):</b> the delayed-keyword group judged more accurately, then chose its weaker texts to reread, and ended with <b>better overall comprehension</b>. It was the first evidence that accuracy affects learning. <b>Thiede et al. (2012)</b> found the same in grades 7 and 8 (see also Rawson et al., 2011). <i>In plain words:</i> judge right, reread right, understand more."),
("So what","The main weak link is the judging, not the rereading. That is exactly what the chapter's advice to teachers (step 7) builds on."),
]
N[(7,0)]=[
("Pictures in texts","Helpful diagrams raised both judgments and scores (Serra &amp; Dunlosky, 2010). Later research found that uninformative pictures raised judgments without raising scores. <b>Ackerman and Leiser (2014):</b> this harm was limited to lower performers; higher performers even profited, possibly because they worked harder to make sense of them. Why such \"seductive details\" (Jaeger &amp; Wiley, 2014) mislead is still open."),
("Paper or screen","<b>Ackerman and Goldsmith (2011):</b> calibration (absolute accuracy) was better on paper than on screen, with the same scores. When students studied at their own pace, paper also led to better regulation and higher scores. Because real study is self-paced, students need extra support when reading on screen."),
("What teachers should do","Pupils already reread sensibly, so teachers need not train the rereading. They should train the <b>judging</b>: be clear about what students should learn, <b>describe the test explicitly</b>, and <b>test deep understanding</b> rather than facts. Students adjust how they read and monitor to the test they expect."),
("More tools for teachers","Teach students to <b>make their own concept maps</b>, promote <b>self-testing later</b> (keywords, summaries, cause-and-effect diagrams), and show how these relate to the test, so students learn which cues predict it. Teachers should also practise good self-regulation themselves."),
]

